our next story takes us to a special  place in africa it's a self-governing  autonomous region with its own currency  military and passport but it is not  recognized as a sovereign state i am  talking about somaliland which broke  away and declared independence from  somalia 30 years ago it's seen as a  stable region especially when compared  to the rest of somalia where there is a  big terrorism threat but most of  somaliland's 4.5 million people live in  poverty  and our east africa correspondent mario  muller has just returned from a  reporting trip uh in somaliland she  joins me now for more mario it is good  to see you i must say i'm envious  because i wonder what was your  impression of somaliland what is life  like that  well as you know somaliland hasn't been  internationally recognized  but when you go there it feels like a  normal state there are normal government  institutions  somaliland has its own flag its own  currency its own military and  somalilanders are also very  proud that somaliland is safe compared  to somalia  where in somalia foreigners like me i  couldn't just move around easily and and  freely as i did now in somaliland so um  that's that's a real difference there  and um of course um people are also very  shaped by the history and the civil war  that happened there three thirty years  ago uh when then rulers here barry had  bombed somaliland and killed tens of  thousands of people so people are still  traumatized the economy is crippled  so the situation is is quite difficult  for most people in somaliland  but there is and also women's rights are  also limited but there's a change  happening actually in in the society in  somaliland and  one especially when it comes to the  rights of women  one shining example is a young woman we  met and you're about to meet now  captain suhor is happy to be at sea  it's where she feels comfortable  steering the somaliland coast guard's  largest vessel  we do patrolling are checking if there's  any  challenges or crimes exist in our sea so  today we are on the mission i like  patrolling because i will learn a lot i  will serve my country to my own duty so  i'm happy  suhoor is the first woman to join the  somaliland coast guard in just four  years she's managed to climb the ranks  to become a captain  it's a remarkable achievement in this  conservative male-dominated society  she's totally capable  she can be on the gun she can steer the  ship  whatever we do she can do it too  their mission is dangerous yemen where a  civil war is raging is just 200  kilometers away  and the somalia-based islamist extremist  group al-shabab poses a constant threat  since the government institutions in  yemen collapsed and our neighbor state  somalia also collapsed they smuggle  weapons  fish illegally traffic humans smuggle  wildlife  so our biggest challenge is coming from  yemen  earlier this year  led a successful operation against  illegal fishing  at home her biggest supporter over the  years has been her sister  she's a success story not only for our  family but for the whole of somaliland  we are very proud to became captain and  leader we wanted to be even more  successful  at 26 suhoor holds three degrees in  international relations from  universities in somaliland and the uk  now she's eyeing a masters in maritime  law since when i was young i was  interested to be part of the security i  was wondering if i can be one of the  high ranks in somaliland  high rank officers so alhamdulillah at  the moment i'm part of them  and you know  i can call see something which is magic  but people on the streets tell us they  don't think women are fit for this work  the military faces many challenges a  woman can't handle it  a woman is responsible for her children  and her house  nothing  else women can't do that job only men  can do their job  in somaliland women have historically  been marginalized the united nations  says there's a prevalence of gender  inequality and violence against women  and that women have limited access to  economic resources and political  representation  but things have started to change more  women's rights advocates female  politicians and activists are making  their voices heard  most of the people who change and who do  a lot of things are women so now i  believe even somalian are lucky because  they have a young graduating female so  they can change at least  she hopes she can be an example for  other young women in somaliland to join  the military  or to follow their dreams  that's incredible  is is an outstanding woman um but we  also heard in your report mariel from  people who don't necessarily agree with  women  being in leadership roles as you  observed it what is the role of women in  somaliland society today  well somaliland society is a  traditionally male-dominated society  with strict religious rules conservative  clan based  so women um they especially in remote  and rural areas they have very limited  access to education to political  participation and to economic resources  there's also violence against women and  that's that's a very big  difficulty  so but now this is just one side of the  story the other side are women like  zohor how you saw her in the report as  other also also other women we met for  example  female politicians women's rights  advocates  female basketball coach or surgeons  so they also these women and many of  them actually  lived abroad for a while studied there  maybe for years or decades and came back  and tried to change somaliland's society  so we also for example filmed in a  nightclub which was run by a woman from  the diaspora community and their women  were showing their hair and dancing and  singing something you would never see on  the streets in somaliland  murray i'm wondering about people's  aspirations in somaliland  what do people want for their future  well what they want for their future is  international recognition of of their  how they call it state um also you heard  um she wants to be a role model she  wants to encourage more young women to  follow in her footsteps and  she also said actually after the  interview well next time you visit me  i'll be a major um and  so you have probably to have this kind  of confidence in order to succeed there  right that's dwc east africa  correspondent murray mullet thank you  mario  you
 

